38 #edb/ref 17592186342070 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342071 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341959 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342198 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342199 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341966 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341967 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341968 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341969 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342152 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342153 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342154 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342072 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342073 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342074 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341872 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341877 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341888 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341970 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341971 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341995 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341998 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342003 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342001 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342002 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341916 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063761 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186064956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341829 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064956 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 9$, where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x = 0$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186064956 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=9,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064956 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =9(1)+C \\\\ C & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=9t-9.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063773 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186064958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341600 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064958 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186064958 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{2}{\\mid x \\mid \\sqrt{4x^{2} - 1}}dx$?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186064958 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2\\,\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}=4\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $2x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342107 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342108 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341908 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341913 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341914 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341915 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341846 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186068924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342064 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342075 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342187 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342188 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342189 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342214 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342215 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342217 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342216 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186073821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342218 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186073304 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341836 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074516 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-1)$ and the line $y = 2$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074516 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-1)$ and the line $y=2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074516 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/f9/f907ee8b0bb92792cc69905678210af71088af6731e5a586eaeed0bfc7856b9c.png)\n\nSince the line $y=2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(5,-1)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-2|=3.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186073322 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341787 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074519 1071 "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y - 3x = 3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074519 1071 "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074519 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186073328 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341832 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074520 1071 "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y =-\\frac{2}{3}x - 2$ that is closest to the point $(2, 1)$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186074520 1071 "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186074520 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186078431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341942 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186078432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341945 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186078432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341951 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186078432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341952 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186078433 1038