ref 17592186341960 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341929 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341961 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341963 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341964 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341965 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342007 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342008 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342004 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342005 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096018 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341807 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097354 1071 "Given that\n$(x + 8)/((x - 2)^{2}) = \\frac{A}{x - 2} + (B)/((x - 2)^{2})$,\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A \\cdot B$?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097354 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+8}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097354 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+8 & =A(x-2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+8 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 10 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+8 & =A(0-2)+10 \\\\ -2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=1\\cdot 10=10.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096024 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341431 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097355 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+2z=8 \\\\  & -2x+2y-3z=-11 \\\\  & x+z=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097355 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x - y + 2z = 8; - 2x + 2y - 3z =-11; x + z = 9$.\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097355 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z=8 \\\\ y-z=1 \\\\ z=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=5$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5)=1\\,⟹\\,y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=6$ and $z=5$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(6)+2(5)=8\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=4$, $y=6$, and $z=5.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096042 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341622 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097358 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-1}{{n}^{3}+1}$ for $n\\ge 1$\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}+3}{3{n}^{2}-n}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097358 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\n1. $a_{n} = \\frac{n - 1}{n^{3} + 1}$ for $n \\ge 1$\n2. $a_{n} = \\frac{2}{n^{3}}$ for $n \\ge 1$\n3. $a_{n} = \\frac{n^{3} + 3}{3n^{2} - n}$ for $n \\ge 1$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097358 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity.\n\n- In sequence I, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-1}{{n}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }⁡\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }⁡\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}+3}{3{n}^{2}-n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence III diverges." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096054 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341727 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097360 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z=1 \\\\ x-y+z=-1 \\\\ 2y-2z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097360 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n${2x - y + z = 1; x - y + z =-1; 2y - 2z = 6$\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097360 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y-z=3 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=3+z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096066 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341683 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097362 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x-y-3z=1 \\\\ 2x+2y+6z=-2 \\\\ -2x-2y-6z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186097362 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n${-x - y - 3z = 1; 2x + 2y + 6z =-2; - 2x - 2y - 6z = 2$\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186097362 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342056 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342057 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342058 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342059 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341975 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341976 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341900 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186100925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341901 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342147 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342148 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342149