ne, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129859 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {x}_{i-1}+2\\cdot {y}_{j-1}\\le 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 4\\cdot {x}_{i}+2\\cdot {y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1}\\le 2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le 2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P).\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2({4}^{2}-{0}^{2})(2-0)=64 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(4-0)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 64+16\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 80\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=80.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128641 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341598 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129865 1071 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2, 5)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129865 1071 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2,5).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129865 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,5)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(2,-2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(2,-2)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2}{2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128653 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341615 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129867 1071 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4, \\frac{π}{6},-2)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129867 1071 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-2).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129867 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128659 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341718 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129868 1071 "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5, π, 7)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129868 1071 "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5,\\pi ,7).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129868 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,0,7).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128665 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341762 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129869 1071 "![](images/a9/a9f9418d0a9984af019254cb06d9b2aab3251dd3127f05b89b118620ac6dd7e2.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A$?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129869 1071 "![](images/a9/a9f9418d0a9984af019254cb06d9b2aab3251dd3127f05b89b118620ac6dd7e2.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129869 1074 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $A$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $A$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi 