 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128689 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341710 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129873 1071 "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1, 2,-\\sqrt{3})$. What are the spherical polar coordinates $(ρ, θ, ϕ)$ of $B$? Express $θ$ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186129873 1071 "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,2,-\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $B?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186129873 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2