e domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](images/cd/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341996 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341997 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341999 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342000 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342009 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341981 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341987 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341990 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341991 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341992 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341993 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341994 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341943 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341944 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341840 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341842 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186175946 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341941 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176102 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341926 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186341950 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175528 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186176570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341759 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176570 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176570 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}e^{x}dx$?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186176570 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175540 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186176572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341831 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176572 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186176572 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^{2}\\ln (t), t \\ge 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186176572 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v({e}^{-3/2}) & =2({e}^{-3/2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-3/2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3/2}(-\\frac{3}{2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184149 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342221 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342060 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342061 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342090 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342062 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342091 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342092 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184357 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342063 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183649 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186184845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341617 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184845 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos u = \\frac{4}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{15}{17}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186184845 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184845 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183655 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186184846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341818 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184846 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{4}{5}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\cos (u + v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{6} - b}{35}$,\ncalculate the value of $a + b$." 13194139533427 17592186045585 false] [17592186184846 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b}{35},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186184846 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}{{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{49}-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{7})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}}{35}-\\frac{15}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-15}{35}\\end{aligne