5585 true] [17592186341838 1077 "selection" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341838 1079 #edb/ref 17592186340647 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341838 1080 #uuid "fe189007-87b5-5c7a-9fdb-38b804f0d731" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1075 #edb/ref 17592186339637 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1075 #edb/ref 17592186339654 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340181 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340679 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340697 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1077 "selection" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1079 #edb/ref 17592186340181 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341839 1080 #uuid "fe1bbcec-fcbd-576d-8667-2c78a327af54" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341822 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1069 "q-72568" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1072 #uuid "99fe5e6a-20b3-53d0-a013-c1c2c0d3ad0c" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341840 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341745 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1069 "q-72570" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 11 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 6 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1072 #uuid "4f7d9000-6b1e-59bb-84e6-86f972afc228" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341841 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$  is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 11 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 6 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341542 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1069 "q-72571" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1072 #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341842 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341452 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1069 "q-72574" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1072 #uuid "c409d434-4c7f-59fa-9d55-4c82bc621cc5" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341843 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition  II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341559 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1069 "q-72576" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1072 #uuid "2d3c090e-cc4f-51d5-a663-f597f7113489" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341844 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341730 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1069 "q-72577" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1072 #uuid "6e33d1be-d21f-5496-9987-25f3728e4904" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341845 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-3$, $4$, and $2$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-3+4+2=3.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341729 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1069 "q-72605" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1071 "$\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1072 #uuid "958efc2b-d91b-53e5-b712-1690bc1b7068" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341846 1074 "Let $u=2-5{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u={x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\cdot {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341469 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1069 "q-72617" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1071 "![](images/d4/d45a9c753270dfd9186bc16736d1815a11997c88d2f35a0c40f487e6acef66c6.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-2,3)$ and the point $X(1,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1072 #uuid "54aa38dd-d17e-53f7-9a6f-f5bb4a2cb026" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341847 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-2))}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341636 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1069 "q-72619" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1071 "![](images/2f/2ff035f38f5892f61d1eb037c7e39c59c8ec5104babac45224c4902152a0cfaa.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-1,-3)$ and the point $X(1,1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1072 #uuid "e9071118-1df3-5b01-93dc-2b5f04fcff39" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341848 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-1))}^{2}+{(1-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{20})^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341543 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1069 "q-72621" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1071 "![](images/6e/6e68ede9db2528ea607fc6fd63f0ec4ca1c1378982239913ef49153e1cf5b609.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(3,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1072 #uuid "b538b794-53e2-55d6-941e-f772138f1dd6" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341849 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{41})^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341516 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1069 "q-72684" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1071 "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=4$ for all partition $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1072 #uuid "3273cb89-af5d-5582-86a1-9111196782fa" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341850 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}4\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =4({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =4(4-0)(6-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=64\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 64=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=64.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341603 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1069 "q-72711" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1072 #uuid "5b1e203a-fce6-56f0-bd3a-5a969f3f611d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341851 1074 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341611 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1069 "q-72712" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1072 #uuid "508dcfe6-ec7a-5fa0-ab23-63e62cd88ad5" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341852 1074 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341507 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1069 "q-72715" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1072 #uuid "dce072f1-1bfb-5e57-9eb5-e624cb48ce45" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341853 1074 "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341482 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1069 "q-72716" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1071 "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-13.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1072 #uuid "5e679b14-a7d9-5eaa-ba4a-ea8d4a8b2fc8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341854 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-13) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341409 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1069 "q-72723" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(B)=91.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{6}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the sixth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1072 #uuid "7b0644d5-53aa-5532-b39e-bdadeb30e9f9" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341855 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(91) \\\\  & =-91.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341447 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1069 "q-72729" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x=-3y \\\\ 2x=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1072 #uuid "ef160bb4-443b-58b5-915b-9dfda073ef96" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341856 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341625 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1069 "q-72731" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=-64.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{8}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1072 #uuid "2858ce5b-8e2a-5132-aac3-acedb511e7f6" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341857 1074 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{5}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot (-64) \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341490 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1069 "q-72733" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=y+1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1072 #uuid "ae55f4eb-c009-586b-a5eb-f77f0f124cd0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341858 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341570 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1069 "q-72738" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x=3y \\\\ y=-x+1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1072 #uuid "cc0bf99b-722a-5c69-a1c9-0059727d8fb4" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341859 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341554 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1069 "q-72739" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(B)=11.$ What is the value of $det(2B)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1072 #uuid "e092bdb9-fca3-5ab8-88ef-c95e1a9b5b5e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341860 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2B) & ={2}^{5}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =32\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =352.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341779 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1069 "q-72740" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(4Y)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1072 #uuid "bce6072f-0fd1-50b6-a305-a94b72b85f4e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341861 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(4Y) & ={4}^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-192.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341753 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1069 "q-72752" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-10.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1072 #uuid "31c24ae4-5a26-5d23-82f7-e8e80b937d7f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341862 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341525 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1069 "q-72763" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ on $M?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1072 #uuid "7de40719-e1b7-5230-a137-1dc3c55de0ef" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341863 1074 "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1+(-1)(1) & -1+(-1)(0) & 1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341802 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1069 "q-72764" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1071 "![](images/f5/f53996727138b9442ac041cf492618db8287b54ba652c4f9e375cdde27dc1862.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1072 #uuid "d86eb6a4-4303-50bf-b47a-fa898e3f8037" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341864 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & ≈53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341546 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1069 "q-72768" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1071 "![](images/36/36af7324fd2112d94feddc5a1a02a91d97bbc0866cb93a6ca711dd73f566305a.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1072 #uuid "c1e965d5-3d0b-5c28-9671-3eb1e1598d0f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341865 1074 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.658,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341664 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1069 "q-72773" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1072 #uuid "47c4e34b-d3a4-5786-b909-c2df73d6cb32" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341866 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/00/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341425 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1069 "q-72779" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1071 "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1072 #uuid "e26e2e63-b096-5a84-a4a3-bdfd754248d4" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341867 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=48.590\\,377{…}^{∘}≈48.590\\,{4}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $48.590\\,{4}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/48/4864e26ba05324041220dff99d1929b4c280b77456295d00d13d246279b4e628.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-48.590\\,{4}^{∘} \\\\  & =131.409\\,{6}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341825 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1069 "q-72782" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1071 "Solve the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1072 #uuid "feffdda0-e317-5f9c-a600-395ea23af899" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341868 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341582 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1069 "q-72786" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $U,$ given by\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $U?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1072 #uuid "4f7e0cb8-b82b-5c57-8695-0b8e08268369" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341869 1074 "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}$ means that we need to multiply the second row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0+0 & -1+1 & -3+0 & 4+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341724 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1069 "q-72824" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-8$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1072 #uuid "5bb6f7b5-fd38-5f44-84f6-733c5b1973e6" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341870 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-8)=-1.446\\,4…≈-1.446\\,4.\n$$\n\nSince $-1.446\\,4$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi -1.446\\,4≈1.695\\,2, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi -1.446\\,4≈4.836\\,8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.695\\,2\\cdot 4.836\\,8≈8.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341608 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1069 "q-72829" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1071 "![](images/6e/6e2223a7dadd9aeddad0b029bf2ba8fc18bafc74dcaca88346ee2b702029b597.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1072 #uuid "e4ff265d-e61a-5c7b-9e6e-b3bd47a9bb05" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341871 1074 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $, hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is  $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341407 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1069 "q-7283" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1072 #uuid "172f42b8-4e20-57a1-8447-38d9c7f98da1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341872 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 15 & =8+4+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341502 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1069 "q-72831" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1071 "![](images/23/23b4f6296c9e840ad90b8235fa66f17e767c61a6cfeac5bc9566fd1fbd35b004.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1072 #uuid "fa1432b6-ebd7-517d-b079-d328cbf0fa84" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341873 1074 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ ." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341760 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1069 "q-72833" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1071 "![](images/aa/aae559883c97bbd19321d9baeb26fe15e47cced5b072e12100c42aa579c9d02d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1072 #uuid "22367997-0344-5325-8db1-e55756e2e128" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341874 1074 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341826 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1069 "q-72835" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1072 #uuid "0c47beb4-7d28-5eec-96a0-29c080da4a45" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341875 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2.300\\,523…≈2.301\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.301$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.301.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.301 & ≈0.841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.841.$\n\n![](images/d0/d02a0250df0f0e6c389594ea0e1b119d9f4c63c353553d56c0e865f487862284.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.841 \\\\  & ≈3.983\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈2.301\\cdot 3.983≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341640 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1069 "q-72838" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{4}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1072 #uuid "40af3b43-8968-5b55-af69-0a7608e19327" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341876 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})=0.962\\,550…≈0.962\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.962\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.962\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2cd7e76526632d8507ba484246a560829882e7f10d9814f01593f7a48d9a2ac.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.962\\,6 \\\\  & =5.320\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈0.962\\,6\\cdot 5.320\\,6≈5.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341590 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1069 "q-7284" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-7$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1072 #uuid "35ab132d-9e7c-5e1c-baf1-f58667f680d8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341877 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(3)=-7.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{1}{8}(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}+C \\\\ -7 & =-\\frac{63}{8}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341547 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1069 "q-72840" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1072 #uuid "3b3398cc-8758-5d62-8796-0ec13a13ea7d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341878 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=1.318\\,116…≈1.318\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.318$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.318.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$\n\n![](images/87/8797bd2b1558e094b72d891fd344b356e5d579e4549b788bdd0507de23f171c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.318 \\\\  & =4.965\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.318\\cdot 4.965≈7\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341726 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1069 "q-72841" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1072 #uuid "9975124e-84e0-58f3-a162-bfccabebefd9" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341879 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{5}) & =1.982\\,313…≈1.982\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.982\\,3$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.982\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.982\\,3 & ≈1.159\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.159\\,3.$\n\n![](images/25/250adbaae0378e3f42ab4340479bb4590aa7f39a1094ea39010de3d5758b4e3e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.159\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.300\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.982\\,3\\cdot 4.300\\,9≈8.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341462 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1069 "q-72842" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1072 #uuid "7784cc80-20e0-55b0-b1c3-2e85b506528d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341880 1074 "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}=-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341784 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1069 "q-72843" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1071 "Solve the equation $2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1072 #uuid "defa6c0d-9130-5253-9c9f-03f2fde86599" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341881 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341748 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1069 "q-72845" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1071 "Find the solution(s) to the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2}=1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1072 #uuid "3dee65bc-3bdf-57d3-9d7b-8a1802789c99" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341882 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341475 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1069 "q-72850" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $U$ into the matrix $V$, where\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1072 #uuid "c70a88ac-8f1d-5e97-afff-9c3f83ff71f2" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341883 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $1$st row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $1$st and the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341477 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1069 "q-72854" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1072 #uuid "4a88dee0-a622-519b-8c88-19f1fcfff46b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341884 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341451 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1069 "q-72856" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $K$ into the matrix $L$, where\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1072 #uuid "dd718a57-6301-5304-b58e-f8632645f2ed" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341885 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341815 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1069 "q-72857" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1071 "Given that\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1072 #uuid "4620d00b-e63c-5bb6-90cb-233c01e9e5ab" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341886 1074 "Of the given options, we consider the row operation    \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 3 & \\,0 & \\,0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341533 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1069 "q-72860" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1071 "Given that\n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1072 #uuid "d64eac74-31dd-5514-b904-fdc9f48abd1b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341887 1074 "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341484 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1069 "q-7287" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1072 #uuid "f823e7c2-6ed4-59db-a566-84d27827d618" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341888 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{12}(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341680 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1069 "q-72905" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1071 "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(3{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}-1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(3{x}_{i}+{y}_{j}-1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1072 #uuid "97e4b704-cbe8-5225-ae99-d2208ea9c6e2" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341889 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}-1\\le 3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1\\le 3{x}_{i}+{y}_{j}-1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n3{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}-1\\le \\frac{3}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1\\le 3{x}_{i}+{y}_{j}-1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le \\frac{3}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{3}{2}({4}^{2}-{1}^{2})(4-2)=45 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})(4-1)=18 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =(4-1)(4-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 45+18-6\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 57\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=57.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341799 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341890 1069 "q-72906" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341890 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-1]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and 