ing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{5}{2}((-1)^{2}-(-5)^{2})(3-0)=-180 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({3}^{2}-{0}^{2})(-1-(-5))=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le -180+36\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le -144\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-144.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341593 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1069 "q-72909" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1071 "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [1,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=-2$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1072 #uuid "0bb13f15-26ed-5a73-9986-ed09f0c80e0e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341891 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-2)\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =-2({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =-2(4-1)(4-1) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=-18\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le -18=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-18.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341654 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1069 "q-72914" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1072 #uuid "0e89bb93-f1e6-5d54-9eb6-f01d87f935ca" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341892 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341568 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1069 "q-72916" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-12y+35=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1072 #uuid "2e0e7a69-7491-597a-b41b-351c429eaf9f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341893 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2(0)-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ (y-6)^{2}-36+35 & =0 \\\\ (y-6)^{2} & =1 \\\\ y-6 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =6\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =5,\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5)$ and $(0,7).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341426 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1069 "q-72939" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1071 "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+20x-12y=-28.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1072 #uuid "6f0d0009-b766-5be4-a470-30d32f4056c3" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341894 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+20x-12y & =-28 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}+20x)+(2{y}^{2}-12y) & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}+20x)+(2{y}^{2}-12y) & =-28 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+4x)+2({y}^{2}-6y) & =-28 \\\\ 5[(x+2)^{2}-4]+2[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =-28 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}-20+2(y-3)^{2}-18 & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}-20+2(y-3)^{2}-18 & =-28 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+2(y-3)^{2} & =-28+20+18 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+2(y-3)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,3).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341711 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1069 "q-72972" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1072 #uuid "e6fb7638-5059-526d-b459-d78ea32e04f3" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341895 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y+z=-8 \\\\  & y+2z=-1 \\\\  & z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1-4=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(-5)+(2)=-8\\,⟹\\,x=-8+10-2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-5$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-5,2⟩.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341567 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1069 "q-72978" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1072 #uuid "56c99816-35f0-58d4-84c1-8bf6a92e3e72" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341896 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+3=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)+4(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3+1+4=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341835 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1069 "q-72980" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y-z=2 \\\\  & y+3z=6 \\\\  & -z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1072 #uuid "d6ce04cb-fc7f-5c70-94bf-242e6df7387a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341897 1074 "The third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=6\\,⟹\\,y=6-9=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-3$ into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n2x+(-3)-(3)=2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(2+3+3)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=-3$ and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,-3,3⟩.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341767 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1069 "q-72981" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1072 #uuid "41fa8809-57ef-578c-b39f-9d0b73e06f90" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341898 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+z=2 \\\\  & 3y+2z=-1 \\\\  & -z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=2\\,⟹\\,z=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3y+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(-1+4)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-2$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,x=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=1$, and $z=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341606 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1069 "q-72983" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1072 #uuid "6577a967-44f3-50e6-9603-cd6d17cab94e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341899 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y+z=-1 \\\\  & y+z=1 \\\\  & 2z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n \n\n$$\n2z=1\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=y=\\frac{1}{2}$  into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})=-1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341579 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1069 "q-72992" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $p(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-3)=-6,\\,{f}^{′}(-3)=2,\\,p(-3)=-5,\\,{p}^{′}(-3)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))$ at $x=-3.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1072 #uuid "0e04fc55-212a-5a19-b8be-ebbd50da7e8a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341900 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))={f}^{′}(x)+{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-3$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))|}_{x=-3} & ={f}^{′}(-3)+{p}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =2+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341393 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1069 "q-72996" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1071 "For the functions $e(x)$ and $n(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\ne(-5)=5,\\,{e}^{′}(-5)=-6,\\,n(-5)=-7,\\,{n}^{′}(-5)=9\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))$ at $x=-5.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1072 #uuid "8bd63bec-2f2e-5dc7-9fc2-4882af93d878" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341901 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))=3{e}^{′}(x)+{n}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))|}_{x=-5} & =3{e}^{′}(-5)+{n}^{′}(-5) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+9 \\\\  & =-18+9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341524 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1069 "q-72998" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1071 "![](images/6d/6d5146afb9508532145180a4f632c0b5c4495487b0a9a89cfd4f71e134b95f50.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1.5).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1072 #uuid "241c4369-248c-546e-84df-6e2b47d7a0e2" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341902 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-2,1].$ Notice that $x=-1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/22/2216d3014a8c74102d5e298c300092a575798de0b66d89914716898bc16ac655.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341566 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1069 "q-73015" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}{2x+4}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1072 #uuid "f02481a9-b93c-590a-b202-053a18900f95" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341903 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}{2x+4} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x+2)}{2(x+2)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/3a/3ad83a79ccd47d9d035ac5a3f47b929a1e6b3939cdaddee9ff0f2d6a3cf07e56.png)" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341501 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1069 "q-73029" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x-6}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1072 #uuid "80a51b01-2c2b-56a0-b939-41609f610e3e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341904 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x-6} & =\\frac{x-6-(x+3)}{(x+3)(x-6)}=-\\frac{9}{(x+3)(x-6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-6)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\\,,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-3$ and $x=6.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341643 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1069 "q-73031" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1072 #uuid "d13dc12b-246a-57c9-9dc3-693e68fcfaf7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341905 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{x+1+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{2x}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=1$ and $x=-1.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341399 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1069 "q-73046" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1071 "![](images/ad/adec7c5c587dcaf899d53432f724a52c0b59d40dfc9d5fafc6cfca92ca3e052e.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1072 #uuid "a3e4972d-5980-5bfd-b465-951c20e0efd0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341906 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-1$ only." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341571 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1069 "q-7305" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1071 "Express $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1072 #uuid "188c3216-6730-58aa-bfa7-8985bab845cd" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341907 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341639 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1069 "q-73054" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1071 "![](images/a0/a025dc971bd0bb473e6b47f90b68ee8e8573a7acc8e5e879eab0173be1d41dcb.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above left-continuous but is not continuous?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1072 #uuid "76c676d7-e91d-5120-9556-9fa39fe68f12" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341908 1074 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-1$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne -1=f(-1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341633 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1069 "q-73064" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1071 "If $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1072 #uuid "bac49aac-db35-5de6-9958-1ba30afe81ea" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341909 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=3{x}^{3}+9x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{3}+9y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{3}+9y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =9{y}^{2}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{9{({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341699 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1069 "q-73071" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1071 "![](images/f8/f8ec0a6a96e74958d1972cea2778b9c974abfad08f2457bc8f2a9f6c3778db15.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(-2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1072 #uuid "66744c9b-eb26-5db6-8152-9819040359e0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341910 1074 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot h(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=2$ and $h(-2)=-2.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(-2)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot h(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341575 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1069 "q-73074" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1071 "If $p(x)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find ${({p}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${p}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1072 #uuid "e62dc2c9-3155-5968-a118-3c566a7e8d10" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341911 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2y+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={p}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal. Notice that we will use of the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2y+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+1)\\cdot 2+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4y+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4y+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${p}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={p}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{p}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)} \\\\ {({p}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341521 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1069 "q-73083" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1071 "If $g(x)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1072 #uuid "0a8f8a40-9792-5bd1-b9f8-2d94ebc38d37" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341912 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡2y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341504 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1069 "q-73084" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1071 "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-2,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x}?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1072 #uuid "04be1a7f-cd78-5782-b9ff-4896edb18e9d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341913 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341716 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1069 "q-73105" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1071 "![](images/ad/ad3fb8a0cce2a6dac706d7876aca0a2339fe49c05cb7aa6ae54de86735ceafab.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is continuous at $x=3$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1072 #uuid "79464211-37d1-5cac-b164-284bb9d9308a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341914 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341775 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1069 "q-73106" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1071 "![](images/89/89dca4b059ccbd1fe93d000acabcbd33b5fc10389de48ef3cbae65cb4455f29e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1072 #uuid "1f47bb47-6168-5cb8-92db-b7a3fc493275" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341915 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341736 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1069 "q-73134" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=2{t}^{3}+{e}^{2t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1072 #uuid "8590b1ed-f003-5495-aa4c-c3e837bd83ad" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341916 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341392 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1069 "q-73143" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1072 #uuid "9b6b5339-4232-5551-9dd0-10e8019e2d36" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341917 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $4$ and $-1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $4+(-1)=3.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341830 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1069 "q-73145" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1072 #uuid "a5a8f2f1-6cb7-517c-b610-e099919e39b1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341918 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $-3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+(-3)=-2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341535 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1069 "q-73146" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1072 #uuid "bdbedd00-251c-5d62-8d5d-e1f5aafe1279" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341919 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $6$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+6=7.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341827 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1069 "q-73148" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1072 #uuid "150469bf-3b37-5806-a5a4-fe5648ae21a5" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341920 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-7$, $5$, and $3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-7+5+3=1.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341797 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1069 "q-73149" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1072 #uuid "5da4a360-a207-5df6-9d38-99e7b8b5a765" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341921 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{31}$ and ${a}_{42}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341723 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1069 "q-73151" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1072 #uuid "60f78bd4-1ce7-5e77-a7ec-044b223be147" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341922 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1069 "q-73154" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1072 #uuid "b1197e9c-9cbb-5cfc-8f5c-c95842ee4a3f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341923 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{41}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341681 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1069 "q-73160" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1072 #uuid "9f9a39ce-97fb-5b82-8335-1f0d956000c1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341924 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341648 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1069 "q-73185" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1072 #uuid "d15a7348-cc49-5a9d-8853-862dbb5ad2b7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341925 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡(4x-3),$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+m\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(4(1+\\frac{5m}{n})-3)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341777 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1069 "q-73188" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+8,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time when the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1072 #uuid "70e0bb5d-6e7c-5325-b428-f7672162cc8e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341926 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+8) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=1.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=1$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)+8 \\\\  & =1-2+8 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $7.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341390 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1069 "q-73192" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ between $x=1$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1072 #uuid "6144fe4d-1170-577e-833f-34f148107f4b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341927 1074 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341454 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1069 "q-73195" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-2}{(x+3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+3}-\\frac{17}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1072 #uuid "3df5e280-10bc-5c40-be58-baa686dafca8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341928 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+3)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2 & =A(x+3)-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)-2 & =A(0+3)-17 \\\\ -2 & =3A-17 \\\\ 15 & =3A \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341444 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1069 "q-73202" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-6}{(x-9)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-9}+\\frac{B}{(x-9)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1072 #uuid "f5cf1db8-874d-5ac0-afa9-a9aa39b25f81" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341929 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-9)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6 & =A(x-9)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=9$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(9)-6 & =A(9-9)+B \\\\ 21 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)-6 & =A(0-9)+21 \\\\ -6 & =-9A+21 \\\\ 9A & =27 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=3\\cdot 21=63.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341800 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1069 "q-73206" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1072 #uuid "cd3eca7c-e429-545d-b746-4c275aedc726" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341930 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =A(x-2)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-2)-2 \\\\ -4 & =-2A-2 \\\\ 2A & =2 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341811 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1069 "q-73237" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1071 "${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1072 #uuid "784294da-08c1-5f80-a6f1-add45f71373b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341931 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{64}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341776 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1069 "q-7324" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1071 "Express $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1072 #uuid "ff03781e-e811-57da-88cb-583d049585d0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341932 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={30}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {30}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341675 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1069 "q-73249" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1071 "![](images/ec/ec8101640f2bd84506d1c47b92f088675080d87eafff4000a4f3e0d14ccebb55.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-2$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1072 #uuid "9eb34e90-018e-520a-8053-32b72e25bff0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341933 1074 "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ shows a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341646 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1069 "q-73250" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1071 "![](images/a8/a8a70a2f28585b63d2a083b42eb6ad54bfb581b5f13c699a3bc3c8d836afaee8.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1072 #uuid "fa88d46b-d2ce-5164-bbed-10d691e29ef6" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341934 1074 "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341472 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1069 "q-73252" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1071 "![](images/1f/1f2d8b9faac6c300b41902cea5065bf8bafb02c137232ee79779a4dc3ff67f33.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1072 #uuid "fb7ec702-6570-50d7-8199-a59c190c3e40" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341935 1074 "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ So, it can't be differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III  are true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341663 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1069 "q-73257" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the plane $z-y=3$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1072 #uuid "ee3d8721-374e-5615-9e25-19f56535791f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341936 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y=3\\,⟹\\,z=y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{({y}^{2}+2y+1)+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341714 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1069 "q-73260" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1072 #uuid "ef354870-3599-55d1-9c3b-8b4fa57a2d1f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341937 1074 "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341731 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1069 "q-73264" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1071 "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=1?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1072 #uuid "5ee6feec-8c7a-50ad-8491-2db169d28d30" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341938 1074 "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>-1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1$, and hence it is not differentiable there." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341613 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1069 "q-73267" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1072 #uuid "7575eddd-8de6-52ad-9db9-422a74ac2262" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341939 1074 "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341540 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1069 "q-73270" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1072 #uuid "362e198f-bd1f-525b-82d3-de496c41fcc9" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341940 1074 "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341657 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1069 "q-73272" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1071 "${\\int }_{-3}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1072 #uuid "a3061017-2ee1-5e02-9c70-56a31206bf16" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341941 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{0}-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341527 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1069 "q-73282" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1072 #uuid "f06a0425-ddb6-5fb1-9198-ac3bc02137e9" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341942 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341563 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1069 "q-73286" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1072 #uuid "41bab868-1060-5798-adf0-32f97366917b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341943 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341439 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1069 "q-73289" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1071 "${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1072 #uuid "98dd252e-cda8-57e3-b4c8-968d1e2d356e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341944 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341788 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1069 "q-73319" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{5}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$, calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}(f(x)+4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1072 #uuid "a7f6cfb9-ceee-5b0e-82db-0c2c9040b028" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341945 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{5}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factoring out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(f(x)+4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{1}^{5}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =2+2({5}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+48 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341653 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1069 "q-73324" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1071 "![](images/33/333a99cd7b1f4aa0c3017c2b5aa42cb04acad842b19345a1176d57a71c5fd120.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-{sec}^{2}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Find the area of the region." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1072 #uuid "9cc7964b-3169-5f22-8d75-6a7a05d33c9b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341946 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341635 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1069 "q-73327" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1071 "![](images/70/70a4f7ef7589f303924f1c48e7b0c26cd2173960847ac660199d11ae144c0afc.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Find the area of the region." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1072 #uuid "5a57d1c4-bb4c-5c4c-98eb-5c77a7edaff8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341947 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\sqrt{2}-1.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341764 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1069 "q-73329" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1071 "![](images/d7/d787ab7b213815ceb2cee11cf3f4a629c0f24b8328b33ae773be1e63f9f9a517.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=1$, as shown above. What is the area of the region?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1072 #uuid "c981b646-bf72-5637-bcb5-fa81d4699700" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341948 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{-2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341400 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1069 "q-73333" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1071 "![](images/18/1819bad0219f1709a01100daad00beb25a8bbc3688f9fe7c41be53bad8010455.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-0.5$ and $x=0.5$, as shown above. What is the area of the region?" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1072 #uuid "091e393b-fab2-5cbe-beb6-54d3099ab9eb" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341949 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-0.5}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.5)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.5)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341670 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1069 "q-73335" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1072 #uuid "d92532a9-3747-54ab-9751-de7dafe084bf" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341950 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341488 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1069 "q-73339" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{5}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$, calculate\n${\\int }_{3}^{5}(5-4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1072 #uuid "a4742c78-15b1-5291-9870-cc1b1bd2e9f1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341951 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{5}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factoring out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}(5-4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x{|}_{3}^{5}-4(5) \\\\  & =5(5-3)-20 \\\\  & =10-20 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341423 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1069 "q-73340" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4$, calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}(7f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1072 #uuid "fdb8c5b5-ac78-5047-b7ca-49e21f3470c3" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341952 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(7f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7(4)+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =28+2(3-1) \\\\  & =28+4 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341551 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1069 "q-73341" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(2f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1072 #uuid "915a0b75-2072-50f8-bd9c-bb7ffa459a6d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341953 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+6={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8={\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{7}(2f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(8)+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{3}^{7} \\\\  & =16+3(7-3) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341522 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1069 "q-73343" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{7}({x}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1072 #uuid "182c5159-b068-5ad4-8a44-92bc06da3c02" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341954 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{7}({x}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{7}(-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341523 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1069 "q-73347" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1071 "![](images/ff/ff919b53932bf52cdfb896ba528eccace648e4b840af1d1cd495299fe1b09119.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-4,2]$ and differentiable on $(-4,2).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,2)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1072 #uuid "f739faa8-8771-50e2-bef5-64730934141d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341955 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-4)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{2+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,2)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}.$*" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341758 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1069 "q-73349" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1071 "![](images/63/6393810d90b8d340dbde041c43cb7e146cb59fedfcfd4a678827d291ea502b00.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,4]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,4).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1072 #uuid "af62622e-80ea-518f-a1c6-117cefb012fa" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341956 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-5)}{4-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}.$*" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341599 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1069 "q-73350" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1072 #uuid "894941c4-4ec1-5dfe-8658-9b67ea8938f5" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341957 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+\\frac{1}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{16}{3}={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(3f(x)+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3})+3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =16+3(6-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341813 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1069 "q-73354" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1071 "![](images/02/029a51dccba9f1ec927c820fb8adb72e926b88a881f4646090ac651ef29da271.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on $(-5,3).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1072 #uuid "9a499767-f41c-56e0-84b5-3017a6ea8366" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341958 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}.$*" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341645 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1069 "q-73361" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1072 #uuid "47640681-1f63-58a5-b2bd-140af66b43bb" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341959 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341810 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1069 "q-73375" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-5}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-5}+\\frac{B}{(x-5)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1072 #uuid "a857dcff-7c63-50d0-a2c4-d8978d06c099" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341960 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-5)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =2(x-5)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=5$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)-5 & =2(5-5)+B \\\\ 10-5 & =2(0)+B \\\\ 5 & =0+B \\\\ B & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341792 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1069 "q-73377" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+4}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1072 #uuid "03cd2ef2-b67c-5c83-9617-ab1026b26f70" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341961 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =A(x+4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+7 & =A(-4+4)+B \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+7 & =A(0+4)+3 \\\\ 7 & =4A+3 \\\\ 4 & =4A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341465 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341962 1069 "q-7338" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186341962 1071 "Express $8{cos}^{2}⁡{