me{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)}{2}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)}{2}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341402 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1069 "q-74088" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1071 "$\\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1072 #uuid "b94b38f3-a670-562e-93ec-c45c6d3b0d77" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342153 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (6x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(6x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341795 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1069 "q-74090" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1071 "Calculate $\\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1072 #uuid "31ac68e1-bc05-5634-b712-63c327f4e3c2" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342154 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=10x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=10 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 10x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10x\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-10\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341688 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1069 "q-74092" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1071 "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1072 #uuid "4f8104e5-6add-51bc-bb53-033c9c000e69" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342155 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k+(-3)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2k-9-4+5 \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341796 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1069 "q-74094" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1071 "Given that $‖v‖=6$ and $u\\cdot v=-9$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1072 #uuid "07e2bb41-0c49-51fe-8a87-09ce22203900" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342156 1074 "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-9| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot 6 \\\\ 9 & \\le 6\\cdot ‖u‖ \\\\ 3 & \\le 2\\cdot ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3 & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot ‖u‖ \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\le ‖u‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$\nis  $\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341806 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1069 "q-74105" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1071 "Let $A(1,-3,2,-1)$ and $B(1,1,3,-2).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1072 #uuid "8d38c4f2-4cfd-5f2e-9ed7-b5d4ad5c5c7d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342157 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{15}\\cdot \\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6÷3}{15÷3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341436 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1069 "q-74106" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1071 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖=4$ and $‖3u+2v‖=17.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1072 #uuid "cf8db75e-23e5-5bab-a06d-d116f90cecd1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342158 1074 "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $3u$ is orthogonal to $2v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3u+2v{‖}^{2} & =‖3u{‖}^{2}+‖2v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3u+2v{‖}^{2} & =(3‖u‖)^{2}+(2‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖3u+2v{‖}^{2} & =9‖u{‖}^{2}+4‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (17)^{2} & =9‖u{‖}^{2}+4(4)^{2} \\\\ 289 & =9‖u{‖}^{2}+4\\cdot 16 \\\\ 289 & =9‖u{‖}^{2}+64 \\\\ 225 & =9‖u{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖u‖=5.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341692 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1069 "q-74109" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1071 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖u‖=4$ and $‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v‖=\\sqrt{182}.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖v‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1072 #uuid "1a630748-fd4b-5158-b450-f22f5e9272e5" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342159 1074 "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $\\sqrt{2}u$ is orthogonal to $\\sqrt{3}v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =‖\\sqrt{2}u{‖}^{2}+‖\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2}‖u‖)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =2‖u{‖}^{2}+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (\\sqrt{182})^{2} & =2(4)^{2}+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =2\\cdot 16+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =32+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 150 & =3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖v{‖}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖v‖=5\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341735 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1069 "q-74110" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1071 "Let $A(0,-2,1,-3)$ and $B(-1,2,2,-3).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1072 #uuid "b674ce78-18bf-5ff8-a526-99d8937fa2a3" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342160 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341398 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1069 "q-74114" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1071 "Given $d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖d‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1072 #uuid "606b6534-faef-5a79-b61f-d54f08761ae1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342161 1074 "Using the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖d‖ & =\\sqrt{{d}_{1}^{2}+{d}_{2}^{2}+{d}_{3}^{2}+{d}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341519 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1069 "q-74117" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1071 "Given $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖v‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1072 #uuid "9a189b1e-6084-525e-b3be-a2438685ab6a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342162 1074 "Using the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}+{v}_{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1+1+4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341397 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1069 "q-74121" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1071 "Let $A(-1,3,2,-4)$, $B(1,0,1,-2)$, and $C(0,1,-1,2).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠BAC.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1072 #uuid "9648ac8e-31b7-5b3c-8ab2-e726d436ab1f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342163 1074 "Let $v=\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $w=\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =b-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =c-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $\\theta =m∠BAC$ be the angle between the vectors $v$ and $w.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341595 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1069 "q-74126" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1071 "Find the distance between the vector $c=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $0.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1072 #uuid "af2154e6-d3f8-5a48-b875-56e7eea54063" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342164 1074 "Using the formula for the distance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(c,0) & =‖c-0‖ \\\\  & =‖c‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{3}^{2}+{c}_{4}^{2}+{c}_{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+0+4+16+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341833 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1069 "q-74128" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1071 "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖-2u‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1072 #uuid "7e06b7e6-fc39-5b93-a36f-ce6fa273939e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342165 1074 "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖ku‖=|k|\\cdot ‖u‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact and the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2u‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖u‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}+{u}_{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4+1+25+9+1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341548 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1069 "q-74131" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1071 "Given the vector $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖-\\frac{w}{5}‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1072 #uuid "2c5e4c59-1c43-52e6-80bc-95dc392cac10" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342166 1074 "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kw‖=|k|\\cdot ‖w‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact and the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\frac{w}{5}‖ & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\cdot ‖w‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4+16+4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341544 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1069 "q-74136" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1071 "If $‖2x‖=1$, find $‖-14x‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1072 #uuid "f10e4697-d07b-5a86-b37b-fc9b133ecd85" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342167 1074 "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kx‖=|k|\\cdot ‖x‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2x‖ & =1 \\\\ |2|\\cdot ‖x‖ & =1 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $‖-14x‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-14x‖ & =14\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341498 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1069 "q-74138" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1071 "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1072 #uuid "03129fa3-21c1-5ca6-84c0-d815ec341170" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342168 1074 "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341497 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1069 "q-74141" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1071 "Let $A(-2,-1,1,-1)$, $B(0,-2,0,1)$, and $C(-1,0,1,-1).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠BAC.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1072 #uuid "b6c0b029-db3b-52de-a19f-9242aebe3a4f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342169 1074 "Let $v=\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $w=\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =b-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =c-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =m∠BAC$ be the angle between the vectors $v$ and $w.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341405 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1069 "q-74157" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1072 #uuid "42a9bb11-63b7-5a50-a482-a6e10afd8275" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342170 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x-1) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x & =(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x=(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341820 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1069 "q-74159" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1071 "![](images/cd/cd6555800f4b7c7a7ae76ef9dd75ebca543ea4898b4205dd24df15f52f95653d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y=-4{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1,$ as shown above." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1072 #uuid "187b544b-b864-5086-a8f9-5b00288fdbcb" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342171 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis.  Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341538 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1069 "q-74162" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1072 #uuid "297ad6da-3f24-5ac9-9662-ff7d0cf1cfec" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342172 1074 "First, we factor our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341658 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1069 "q-74166" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1072 #uuid "7dbb6350-292b-5e35-a0c3-f36b7bfcba3b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342173 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the denominator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341428 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342174 1069 "q-74167" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342174 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${ta