13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341809 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1069 "q-74172" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1071 "![](images/7e/7ecbb53bb87156d11f9a9d8021182cdfb8c0c1749cd028780377bb61f32adf43.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[-3,6]$, is shown above. It consists of the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $3,$ and two line segments. Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,5].$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1072 #uuid "cd8afad0-6e25-593b-9edf-5db6bd394b38" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342176 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-cirle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5-(-3)}\\cdot (\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{16}+\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341474 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1069 "q-74177" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1071 "Given that $‖u+5v‖=19$ and $‖u‖=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1072 #uuid "fdde46c8-c8ef-58e5-8db0-8677a8608fd1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342177 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+5v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖5v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+5v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+5‖v‖ \\\\ 19 & \\le 4+5‖v‖ \\\\ 15 & \\le 5‖v‖ \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$ is $3.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341505 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1069 "q-74180" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1072 #uuid "7987f3f5-5816-5c61-828f-b358bfeacc01" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342178 1074 "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot w-(2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot (2w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+2(u\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-2(-4+0+0-1)+(4+0-6-5) \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341552 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1069 "q-74181" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal. Find $(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1072 #uuid "f2fb4e33-4677-5c93-bb5f-be059ac583ba" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342179 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w) & =(2u)\\cdot v+(3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =2(u\\cdot v)+3(w\\cdot v)+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+3(w\\cdot v)+0-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(w\\cdot v)-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -(0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =3(0+2+0+-2)-(0-1-9-4) \\\\  & =0-(-14) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341627 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342180 1069 "q-74189" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342180 1071 "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{