t ‖v‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4(‖v{‖}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =13‖v{‖}^{2}+36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62 & \\le 13‖v{‖}^{2}+36 \\\\ 26 & \\le 13‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $2\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{2}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341720 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1069 "q-74192" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1072 #uuid "ce497845-60f6-5e53-8285-767276f5e787" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342182 1074 "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 5+5\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -4+a+5b-10 & =0 \\\\ a+5b & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-b+5 & =0 \\\\ 4a-b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a+5b=14 \\\\ 4a-b=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-4$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21b=-63\\,⟹\\,b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+5(3) & =14 \\\\ a+15 & =14 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=-1-3=-4.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341705 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1069 "q-74193" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1071 "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}=158$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=5$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1072 #uuid "9d2e3d36-9fc7-5c7d-b772-d681465a77e4" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342183 1074 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & ={|u\\cdot v|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =27‖v{‖}^{2}+50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & \\le 27‖v‖+50 \\\\ 108 & \\le 27‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $4\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $2\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341641 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1069 "q-74195" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1072 #uuid "10336818-08ce-508e-8d26-edc0039fd630" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342184 1074 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+b\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ a+0+b-2 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ -a+0+2b+5 & =0 \\\\ -a+2b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+b=2 \\\\ -a+2b=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the first equation to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3b=-3\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(-1) & =2 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=3-(-1)=4.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341549 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1069 "q-74196" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1071 "![](images/3a/3a919c1ad631af6dfea304db2bdf0ad36fcbff6162f516e8591d7ce9cef3ecca.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1072 #uuid "6628a10e-0b07-5061-bbd3-ef23eb0ebd24" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342185 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/c0/c06f6a453581797596203059e6a1bfd30afd959f469f0408889c6c947a368809.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${P}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${P}^{′}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=1$ because $P$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341418 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1069 "q-74198" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1071 "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1072 #uuid "efbc99ee-782d-5cfc-bd9c-e3ca79e0d68e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342186 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=1.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341742 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1069 "q-74202" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-4)$ and the line $x=4.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1072 #uuid "79f2f5ac-44c5-5b82-9c80-2524e0bd699c" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342187 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/d4/d46a6ed76af5c226e327ba197fe019df0d9085e095ca4781898aba54268a1706.png)\n\nSince the line $x=4$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-1,-4)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $4.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-4|=5.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341560 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1069 "q-74206" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,-3)$ and the line $y=5.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1072 #uuid "ac97e72e-819e-5e12-8c93-f28e59156bf1" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342188 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/2a/2a3107d0db934d4dc9552444badc52914f92d46d7fd83a3e6048d06f3465dcf9.png)\n\nSince the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(2,-3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-3-5|=8.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341494 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1069 "q-74207" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,3)$ and the line $y=-2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1072 #uuid "7b9cba20-3226-5f81-9b16-00c0c15c6053" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342189 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/67/67f63247ff1f91fae17f257512849ac2415a3894d57475d1e8a7afee1094df31.png)\n\nSince the line $y=-2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-3,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $-2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|3-(-2)|=5.\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341453 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1069 "q-74209" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k-3\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(B).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1072 #uuid "51a492c4-1836-535a-a084-317ef0e2317c" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342190 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(B),$ then the equation $Bx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & k-3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & k-3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k+2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=k-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341769 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1069 "q-74212" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $L=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k-11 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(L).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1072 #uuid "035f63e6-ef7b-5c17-ba6e-584735b13fed" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342191 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(L),$ then the equation $Lx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[L\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & k-11 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -10 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 1 & k-11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1 & k-11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k+4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10 \\\\ 0=k-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-16,$ which means that $k=16.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341448 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1069 "q-74213" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $M=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2k+10\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(M).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1072 #uuid "5a3c8192-bc53-5c3d-a7c3-d44d3533846a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342192 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ then the equation $Mx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 2k+10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 15 & 2k+10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2k-10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 0=2k-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=2k-10,$ which means that $k=5.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341702 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1069 "q-74217" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0}$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1072 #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342193 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341834 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1069 "q-74218" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1072 #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342194 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341479 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1069 "q-74223" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1071 "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2}+{x}_{3}{m}_{3},$ and ${m}_{1}$, ${m}_{2}$, and ${m}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1072 #uuid "3ab37f17-4ea0-5e43-8d25-5328ba6ba1aa" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342195 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -13 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=10 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2}+2(-1) & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-2 & =10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+(-4)-5(-1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+1 & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${m}_{1},$ ${m}_{2},$ and ${m}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{1}}+(-4)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-1)=2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341665 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1069 "q-74233" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1071 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1072 #uuid "e2807df9-570c-5cdc-8ae4-f1ba4e76a273" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342196 1074 "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$1$\n$1$\n$1$\n$-5$\n$3$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x-1{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341780 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1069 "q-74255" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0}$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1072 #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342197 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341756 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1069 "q-74279" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{{sin}^{2}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1072 #uuid "a6ad08b6-febc-5a5b-8528-78fb972c420b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342198 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{{sin}^{2}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡4x\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡4x\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341812 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1069 "q-74281" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1072 #uuid "504ab9d3-58bf-5555-aee6-cd8fa47fea51" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342199 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341750 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1069 "q-74318" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is upper triangular\n- $C$ is lower triangular\n- ${C}^{T}$ is upper triangular" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1072 #uuid "e68d4b2f-c3b6-584c-8430-662a571e8316" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342200 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of $C$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $C$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since ${C}^{T}$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341704 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1069 "q-74319" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is upper triangular\n- $D$ is lower triangular\n- ${D}^{T}$ is upper triangular" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1072 #uuid "10915988-162b-5dec-9de2-5241d5fc3202" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342201 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $D$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $D$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of ${D}^{T}$ are zeros:\n\n$$\n{D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341417 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1069 "q-74348" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1072 #uuid "dc8544c2-610f-58ff-bd57-2903f678a6bb" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342202 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341420 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1069 "q-74398" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1071 "Calculate $\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1072 #uuid "319e0a68-f800-5f78-baec-4e9220398ab8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342203 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341695 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1069 "q-74400" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1071 "$\\int 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1072 #uuid "ea8beebd-1dd9-5d27-af0f-4c6b17fc6bdd" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342204 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341468 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1069 "q-74401" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1071 "$\\int (4-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1072 #uuid "2ba00ff4-d279-5b41-8400-999b5841e29f" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342205 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =4x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341772 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1069 "q-74402" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1071 "$\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1072 #uuid "53ef3692-4956-5314-a757-85cf1b947b82" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342206 1074 "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341460 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1069 "q-74403" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1071 "$\\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1072 #uuid "88de3e26-8c93-5f4c-a03c-1cf27cc2d8b7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342207 1074 "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [3({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)+1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341557 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1069 "q-74404" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1071 "$\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1072 #uuid "2a267544-1d98-5eb4-b631-7909c49f5d74" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342208 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x={cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341694 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1069 "q-74407" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1071 "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1072 #uuid "da6ed9c0-a564-5f50-bd69-a6ceb6be9c50" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342209 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =5x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x={cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10({cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-10(\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341837 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1069 "q-74408" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1071 "Calculate \n$\\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1072 #uuid "4c04929c-0219-5394-9df3-d0e9e41196b7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342210 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [5-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [5-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341828 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1069 "q-74410" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1071 "Calculate $\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1072 #uuid "45475e02-4d34-5bca-af8a-ebf1bb0d0f92" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342211 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341531 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1069 "q-74411" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1071 "Calculate\n$\\int 6{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1072 #uuid "f1e79dd3-b04d-5c24-a841-80ff4b69c7df" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342212 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n2{sin}^{2}⁡3x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\cdot 2{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341432 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1069 "q-74412" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1071 "$\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1072 #uuid "ded7813f-7862-5c34-8d67-a2dd0cf18a45" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342213 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341580 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1069 "q-74417" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1072 #uuid "f0e530ca-0510-53a9-a606-7cbe24765900" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342214 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/eb/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341669 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1069 "q-74420" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1072 #uuid "ac99e412-cc1f-5160-a402-dce6424f38c8" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342215 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341499 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1069 "q-74421" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1071 "![](images/67/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1072 #uuid "a7a18e4a-49f2-563e-8c4d-e70b3f9189cf" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342216 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341791 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1069 "q-74435" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1072 #uuid "5a03ec8c-4418-5e4a-aaf2-129c88104ba7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342217 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/78/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-2 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341588 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1069 "q-74439" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1071 "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1072 #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342218 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341478 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1069 "q-74455" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1071 "![](images/b8/b8f187c949db780dc4ef62360c76be4a952e2c299257c6d4e9ee80c7254605d6.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $W?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1072 #uuid "d626edf9-2c64-5851-ae85-9ffed9b51189" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342219 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d1855e0e8f1b8feed4eb2f5fb9dd2a1abadd0ee92304ae0460f9bd130e376b04.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${W}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${W}^{′}$ is $r=2.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=\\sqrt{3}$ because $W$ lies $\\sqrt{3}$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $W$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341667 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1069 "q-74456" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1071 "![](images/c6/c6de44233dec37e695e70902e0dace0771dd42a4cb87b45205345ba431e7774c.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $K?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1072 #uuid "3ff681b6-e010-5e9c-a510-676867a7f01d" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342220 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/7c/7c49639200c77c264ecb7b58e1eda4053b3ab0fe378d35caa3d3f8bdc6ad5027.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${K}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${K}^{′}$ is $r=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $K$ lies $2$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341529 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1069 "q-74458" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{12}{15},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{9}{15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1072 #uuid "8517f4c3-6d9b-58e0-ad68-ee37cb097540" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342221 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{12}{15})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{30}-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341717 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1069 "q-74460" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1071 "![](images/94/947857af9d5746ceef623a5d2cc2bf0ac82e6570d7f288727a7591278fc50452.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $M?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1072 #uuid "718716c2-5449-5131-9a23-68dc51cb1e8a" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342222 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/88/88aeecb46c1f996a7a2a8947a8b5262faa2991992771a960a1e806c2b8915cac.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${M}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${M}^{′}$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $M$ lies $2$ units *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $M$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341672 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1069 "q-74469" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1071 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1072 #uuid "a02eea4e-b04c-5c03-8b84-ddc1ee505059" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342223 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,1,-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341634 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1069 "q-74474" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1071 "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-3).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1072 #uuid "c21a9421-0e4c-50cb-88ca-b6a37d78a9d4" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342224 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,2,-3).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341442 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1069 "q-74478" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1071 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1072 #uuid "ac7cd288-e5e0-59ab-906b-71d5bba5824b" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342225 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-1,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$ \n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the third  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the reference angle and $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341413 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1069 "q-74479" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1071 "The point $C$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-3,4,-1).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ,z)$ of $C?$ Express $\\theta $ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1072 #uuid "276bbd82-06db-5cb5-9a75-92624054e2f0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342226 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-3,4)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-3,4,-1)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-3,4).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $C$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-3,4)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{4}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.927\\,295 \\\\  & =2.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $C$ are $(5,2.214,-1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341401 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1069 "q-74482" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1071 "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1072 #uuid "79dcf1b7-6a5b-5991-a94b-2a63325fcdf9" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342227 1074 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $P$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341545 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1069 "q-74542" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1071 "![](images/fe/fe4ff907283c9fbce1907651c4047625d750eddfc75f9a9c34f845c3a92b6036.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1072 #uuid "9b07acb0-911c-54b7-8ec2-78f1fd2ecf80" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342228 1074 "Recall that we write the position of a point in spherical coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341789 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1069 "q-74546" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1072 #uuid "b39a9eda-2020-5222-b891-a65118115aa0" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342229 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{a}_{1}+(-2){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=8\\cdot (-2)=-16.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341517 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1069 "q-74550" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1071 "The point $D$ has spherical coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $D.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1072 #uuid "15e1b03e-f124-5d2e-b837-d378c1598893" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342230 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $D$ are $(-1,0,1).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341403 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1069 "q-74551" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1071 "The point $B$ has spherical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1072 #uuid "9904226e-02ba-53bf-837a-e256f030e7c7" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342231 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,\\sqrt{3},3).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341679 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1069 "q-74554" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1071 "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1072 #uuid "52031823-5a5b-5a93-b5b4-e1f6b24e67f3" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342232 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341583 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1069 "q-74555" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1071 "The point $P$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1072 #uuid "2274a373-0a7f-5722-8de2-da35efc5accf" 13194139533427 17592186045585 true] [17592186342233 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\